1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,130
Each map holds five hundred thirty-seven bomb hits.

2
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:06,410
One is pure chance; the other follows a rule.

3
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:08,060
Which is chance?

4
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:17,860
In nineteen forty-four, Londoners claimed flying bombs fell in clusters, and precise aim would put factories at risk.

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:20,630
Build the detector your eye is running.

6
00:00:20,930 --> 00:00:28,360
Cut each map into five hundred seventy-six squares, and flag any square with three or more hits, like this one.

7
00:00:28,966 --> 00:00:32,856
Map A lights up with thirty-nine targets, map B with none.

8
00:00:33,356 --> 00:00:34,666
So A looks aimed.

9
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:36,043
Backwards.

10
00:00:36,643 --> 00:00:42,413
I dropped every hit on map A at random, so all thirty-nine targets are false alarms.

11
00:00:42,713 --> 00:00:46,883
Map B's rule: no two hits closer than four fifths of a square.

12
00:00:47,183 --> 00:00:51,133
Your eye, like the detector, expects chance to look that even.

13
00:00:51,666 --> 00:00:56,006
Each bomb has a one in five seventy-six chance of landing in this square.

14
00:00:56,306 --> 00:00:59,926
To stay empty, all five hundred thirty-seven must miss.

15
00:01:00,366 --> 00:01:02,286
Guess how many squares stay empty.

16
00:01:03,833 --> 00:01:05,593
About two hundred twenty-seven.

17
00:01:05,893 --> 00:01:07,883
Map A has two hundred twenty-two.

18
00:01:08,833 --> 00:01:10,903
Those empty squares force pile-ups:

19
00:01:11,303 --> 00:01:18,753
the other three hundred fifty-four share all the hits, so a hundred eighty-three must land where another already did.

20
00:01:19,053 --> 00:01:22,213
To judge London, chance must predict every bar.

21
00:01:22,666 --> 00:01:25,186
Let's call the average hits per square lambda.

22
00:01:25,686 --> 00:01:32,266
One minus a small slice, raised to a big power, is e to the minus lambda, almost exactly.

23
00:01:32,566 --> 00:01:33,956
Call that P of zero.

24
00:01:34,566 --> 00:01:43,536
For two hits in this square, pick two bombs: five thirty-seven times five thirty-six ways, over two, since order doesn't matter.

25
00:01:44,336 --> 00:01:49,196
Each has that same chance, and the rest miss: e to the minus lambda again.

26
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,880
Five thirty-six is almost five thirty-seven.

27
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:57,240
For three hits, each two becomes a three; for any k, a k.

28
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:02,200
The powers merge into lambda to the k: the Poisson distribution.

29
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:06,950
Times five seventy-six squares, it predicts these purple bars.

30
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:10,560
The honest detector compares whole histograms.

31
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:14,150
Thirty-nine targets, and chance predicts thirty-nine.

32
00:02:14,550 --> 00:02:22,490
The chi-squared misfit squares each bar's gap and divides by chance's bar, so a miss on a short bar counts more.

33
00:02:22,790 --> 00:02:24,010
Map A scores

34
00:02:24,210 --> 00:02:25,180
two point oh.

35
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:29,020
Three out of four simulated chance maps score worse.

36
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:31,850
That share, p, is point seven four.

37
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,310
Now map B: half the expected empty squares, a tower of single hits, misfit two hundred seventy-two.

38
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:44,230
A pattern this even is the fingerprint of a spacing rule.

39
00:02:44,766 --> 00:02:49,836
Aimed bombs would miss the other way: extra empty squares, extra pile-ups.

40
00:02:50,236 --> 00:02:59,606
In nineteen forty-six, R D Clarke published the same test on London's real hits: five thirty-seven in five seventy-six squares.

41
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:06,026
Chance predicted two hundred twenty-seven empty squares; Clarke counted two hundred twenty-nine.

42
00:03:06,326 --> 00:03:08,596
Overall, p equals point eight eight.

43
00:03:09,096 --> 00:03:12,256
London's clusters were no sign of precise aim.

44
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:20,180
The bombs were aimed at central London but tended to fall short, so farther north, hits were about half as dense.

45
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:25,090
The test needs one lambda: Clarke chose an area where it held steady.

46
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,650
The clumps in map A

47
00:03:28,050 --> 00:03:29,950
are what no aim at all looks like.

48
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:31,850
If you chose map B,

49
00:03:32,150 --> 00:03:33,390
you chose the rule.

